[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Flip Animations and List Reordering in Svelte"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Smoothly animating elements when a list is reordered can drastically improve the user experience of a web application. Svelte provides a built-in animation utility called flip (First, Last, Invert, Play) that makes this remarkably easy without needing external animation libraries."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the FLIP Technique"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The FLIP animation technique calculates the initial position of an element (First), the final position after a DOM update (Last), inverts the transform so the element appears to stay in its original spot (Invert), and finally animates the element to its new position (Play). Svelte automates this entire mathematical process."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up the Svelte Component"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To implement list reordering with flip, we need to import the flip function from 'svelte/animate'. Each item in the keyed each block must have a unique identifier so Svelte can accurately track its DOM node."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"<script>
  import { flip } from 'svelte/animate';
  import { cubicOut } from 'svelte/easing';

  let items = [
    { id: 1, text: 'Item A' },
    { id: 2, text: 'Item B' },
    { id: 3, text: 'Item C' }
  ];

  function shuffle() {
    items = items.sort(() => Math.random() - 0.5);
  }
</script>"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying the FLIP Animation in Markup"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, we render the list using Svelte's keyed 'each' block. We apply the animate:flip directive to the list items. Note that keying the each block with (item.id) is mandatory for the flip animation to work."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"<button on:click={shuffle}>Shuffle List</button>

<ul>
  {#each items as item (item.id)}
    <li animate:flip="{duration: 300, easing: cubicOut}">
      {item.text}
    </li>
  {/each}
</ul>"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuration Options for animate:flip"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"duration: Can be a number in milliseconds or a function that returns a duration based on the distance moved."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"easing: An easing function imported from 'svelte/easing' (e.g., cubicOut, bounceOut)."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining keyed each blocks with the animate:flip directive, Svelte handles complex layout transitions declaratively. This approach results in highly performant animations with minimal boilerplate code."}]
